(function () {
  var m = {"DVT210-S": {"abstract": "For a company like Polymarket, moving fast isn’t a nice-to-have. It’s a requirement. Failing to scale quickly enough can stall unprecedented momentum, while compromising its PII and security posture can carry even greater risk. Built on AWS, this session explores how Polymarket uses groundcover to shift infrastructure responsibilities to AI agents, build agentic development loops, maintain full data residency, and gain instant visibility into every change, no matter how quickly production evolves. We’ll also share the immediate impact: at least 50% lower MTTR for infrastructure escalations and an approximately 7% reduction in Polymarket’s AWS bill. This presentation is brought to you by groundcover, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"DVT211-S": {"abstract": "See how Western Governors University uses Bluebox by Dynatrace and AWS Kiro to deliver AI-generated code with greater confidence. By combining live production intelligence with AI-assisted development, teams can provide agents with real runtime context, detect issues quickly, and create evidence-backed GitHub Issues for rapid remediation. Learn how Bluebox supports the software lifecycle before, during, and after deployment, helping teams validate fixes, reduce risk, and accelerate software delivery. This presentation is brought to you by Dynatrace, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"DVT212-S": {"abstract": "CodeRabbit reviews code generated by major coding agents: 30M+ pull requests and 200K+ independent code reviews each day across open source and enterprises. As agentic development accelerated, PR volume grew 4x while review rounds per PR fell 20%. AI-co-authored PRs carry about 1.7x more issues and up to 2.74x more security issues than human-written code. David Loker, CodeRabbit VP of Applied AI, shares 12 months of review telemetry behind 100M caught bugs—and the enterprise pattern it reveals: heterogeneous coding-agent stacks are the end state. He introduces Agentic Change Management: an independent, agent-agnostic quality gate that assesses every change, surfaces what needs human attention, and explains what it approves. Leave with a vendor-neutral framework for scaling an agentic SDLC without sacrificing quality, security, or control. This presentation is brought to you by CodeRabbit, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"DVT213-S": {"abstract": "Ever get a text that your Mastercard was used in a transaction you didn't make? That's because every card swipe has to be evaluated before it's cleared to keep fraud from slipping through. Join Sivakumar Palanasami, Mastercard's EVP of Data Platforms, to learn how his team's real-time fraud decisioning model built with Redis on AWS and for a sneak peek of what they're doing next. This presentation is brought to you by Redis, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"DVT214-S": {"abstract": "Coding agents make sustained, autonomous progress on real software. The question is what happens to the rest of the software development lifecycle. Claude Code runs for hours unattended, works beyond the terminal, and takes on more of the work around the code. We'll show where it stands and how engineering changes when the whole lifecycle becomes automatable: what engineers do, how review and testing evolve, where quality comes from when code is cheap. Live demos on AWS and numbers from our engineering, where Claude writes most of our code. This presentation is brought to you by Anthropic, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"DVT215-S": {"abstract": "Bring AWS's AI-DLC methodology together with open source agents, Amazon Bedrock inference, and a governed Python foundation. Watch a full bolt run live — business intent to deployed AWS workload — with model choice, dependency provenance, and cost attribution intact. AI-native development the enterprise can actually put into production. This presentation is brought to you by Anaconda, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"DVT216-S": {"abstract": "Continuous integration was designed around human pace. A developer pushes a branch, a webhook fires, and a ten minute wait feels acceptable. AI coding agents break that assumption, and validation becomes the longest part of the cycle. Explore how RWX rebuilt the layer underneath continuous integration for agent driven work, including content based caching that skips work already proven unchanged, graph based task execution, a container runtime built for reuse, and a command line interface agents call directly rather than waiting on Git. Learn which parts of a pipeline are structurally slow and which are simply misconfigured. This presentation is brought to you by RWX, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"DVT217-S": {"abstract": "Code is now abundant. AI agents now write, test and deploy code autonomously; the bottleneck has shifted from generating code to governing what reaches production. Loreli Cadapan, VP of Product at CloudBees, takes you inside the governed agentic software factories that the world’s most regulated engineering organizations are building, where every change, human or agent, is visible, auditable and accountable. The work starts after the code changes: encode existing rules as enforceable policy across your toolchain, keep humans in the loop where it matters, and ship software you can stand behind. Because you write code for a reason, and we make sure that reason becomes true. This presentation is brought to you by CloudBees, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"DVT218-S": {"abstract": "Make a change. Deploy it. Wait. Dig through the logs. Try again. For years, that loop has cost infrastructure engineers countless hours—time that could be spent shipping what comes next. In this session, discover how to take engineers out of the routine operational loop without giving up oversight. Define the outcome, let AI work through the iterations, and step back in only when a real decision needs you. See the pattern in action with Terraform, Vault, Kubernetes, Amazon Bedrock, AgentCore, and more. This presentation is brought to you by HashiCorp, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"DVT219-S": {"abstract": "AI is moving beyond the cloud, requiring developers to deploy intelligent workloads across data centers, edge infrastructure, vehicles, industrial systems, PCs, and mobile devices. The challenge is scaling AI efficiently across diverse hardware environments. In this session, learn how Qualcomm and AWS enable developers to build agentic AI applications across the compute continuum, from cloud training to real-time edge and on-device inference. Discover how open frameworks, portable AI runtimes, and streamlined workflows accelerate development and deployment. Through examples spanning automotive, IoT, industrial edge, mobile, datacenter, and cloud use cases, explore how a unified developer experience reduces complexity, increases flexibility, and helps deliver scalable AI applications anywhere. This presentation is brought to you by Qualcomm, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"DVT220-S": {"abstract": "Autonomous agents are no longer assisting the software development lifecycle. They are rewriting it. When agents write, review, test, and ship code at machine speed, the SDLC stops being a sequence of human handoffs and becomes a control problem. This session covers the failure modes that only appear at scale: review queues that no human can absorb, test suites that validate the wrong invariants, provenance gaps that make incidents unexplainable, and CI/CD pipelines built for tens of commits a day now absorbing thousands. It closes with the specific architectural decisions engineering leaders should be making now, drawn from our own experience running agents in production. This presentation is brought to you by Postman, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"DVT221-S": {"abstract": "AI isn't just making software faster; it's changing how software is produced. Engineering teams now run agents that write code, review PRs, validate releases, and deploy around the clock. The promise is real, but so is a new problem: how do you stay in control of systems that never stop evolving? Edith Harbaugh, CEO of LaunchDarkly, argues the defining challenge of the AI era isn't generating code, it's controlling autonomous systems in production, where agents drift and the old detect-fix-redeploy playbook breaks down. She'll share what LaunchDarkly learned building its own AI software factory, and how engineers stay in charge. This presentation is brought to you by LaunchDarkly, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"DVT222-S": {"abstract": "Everyone's talking about AI software factories. Far fewer have actually built one. In this panel, engineering leaders, partners, and teams putting agents into production go past the hype to the real work: where agents accelerate delivery, where they break, and what it takes to keep humans in control as autonomy scales. Expect specifics, not theory—what worked, what didn't, and what they'd do differently. Whether you're experimenting with coding agents or scaling toward autonomous delivery, you'll leave with practical patterns for building a factory that's genuinely fast and safe. Join us for an honest conversation about engineering at agent speed. This presentation is brought to you by LaunchDarkly, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"DVT223-S": {"abstract": "Agents now plan work, write code, generate tests and open PRs—sometimes without human review before production. Yet most teams rely on a trust model built for a slower SDLC: one reviewer, one merge gate and one chance to catch what went wrong. That model fails when agents operate at every stage and final review can’t reveal what happened upstream. This session treats trust as a property of the pipeline, not a final checkpoint. We’ll explore why the system generating code shouldn’t also verify it, how to turn tribal standards into enforceable, auditable controls, and where independent review belongs between spec and deploy. Key takeaways: Why end-stage review fails in an agent-driven SDLC. How to enforce and trace standards across teams, repos and AI tools. How to decide what can merge automatically, what needs human review and how to document that decision for auditors. This presentation is brought to you by Qodo, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"DVT224": {"abstract": "Most production code has no spec. It was written by teams that moved on, and its docs went stale long ago. When you inherit a service, prepare a migration, or chase an incident, you spend days reconstructing what the system does, and your AI agent rediscovers the same behavior in every new chat. We'll first sketch how to generate a structured model of an existing system from its code: its invariants, dependencies, and hidden assumptions. Then it's your turn. Bring your questions and your hardest brownfield problems, and we'll work through them together at the whiteboard.", "language": "en"},"DVT225": {"abstract": "Most production code has no spec. It was written by teams that moved on, and its docs went stale long ago. Before a refactor, migration, or incident fix, developers spend days on code archaeology, and AI agents have to rediscover the same behavior in every new chat with little prior context. Learn how to turn an existing codebase into a structured, persistent model of what the system does: its properties, invariants, component interactions, and hidden assumptions. See how that model helps you onboard faster, baseline behavior before a refactor, and give your AI agent context that's reusable across sessions.", "language": "en"},"DVT226": {"abstract": "Software development happens through teamwork, but agent work is often trapped in individual sessions. The developer who starts the work becomes the coordinator who needs to relay feedback, reconstruct decisions, or keep agents moving. Working with agents as a team has many paper cuts, but there are best practices you can implement. Follow an issue from investigation through implementation, testing, and review, with requests, decisions, and approvals attributed along the way as work progresses. Learn how team-scoped knowledge, reusable practices, and clear permission boundaries let agents take on ongoing responsibilities while people keep control of what ships.", "language": "en"},"DVT227": {"abstract": "How do you let agents carry development work across teammates without handing over control? In this code talk, build an issue-to-review workflow that you can share with your team. Follow the code and configuration for setting up guardrails, permissions, shared workflows, logging, and best practices, so teammates can steer the implementation or review who requested or approved each action alongside the evidence the agent produced. Bring questions and follow along as we explain why each boundary exists and how to adapt the pattern to your toolchain and approval process.", "language": "en"},"DVT301-R": {"abstract": "Every codebase has that annoying pattern: the deprecated internal library, the logging convention nobody follows, the framework migration that keeps getting deferred. In this small-group builder session, you'll tackle yours. Bring a real modernization problem from your own code and build a custom transformation in AWS Transform custom that solves it—defining the transformation in natural language, running it against a repository, and refining it until it holds up. No fixed curriculum and no slides: this is open-ended, hands-on time. Stuck for an idea? We'll have a few ready-made problems on hand. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"DVT301-R1": {"abstract": "Every codebase has that annoying pattern: the deprecated internal library, the logging convention nobody follows, the framework migration that keeps getting deferred. In this small-group builder session, you'll tackle yours. Bring a real modernization problem from your own code and build a custom transformation in AWS Transform custom that solves it—defining the transformation in natural language, running it against a repository, and refining it until it holds up. No fixed curriculum and no slides: this is open-ended, hands-on time. Stuck for an idea? We'll have a few ready-made problems on hand. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"DVT301-R2": {"abstract": "Every codebase has that annoying pattern: the deprecated internal library, the logging convention nobody follows, the framework migration that keeps getting deferred. In this small-group builder session, you'll tackle yours. Bring a real modernization problem from your own code and build a custom transformation in AWS Transform custom that solves it—defining the transformation in natural language, running it against a repository, and refining it until it holds up. No fixed curriculum and no slides: this is open-ended, hands-on time. Stuck for an idea? We'll have a few ready-made problems on hand. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"DVT302-R": {"abstract": "AI now touches every stage of software delivery: coding assistants, autonomous agents, modernization tools, security scanners, operations, and the list grows every week. Which do you actually use, and when? This open discussion helps you make sense of the SDLC tooling landscape. Bring your scenario and we'll talk through it together: when to hand work to an autonomous agent versus a deterministic tool, how to chain AI across design, code, review, and operations without creating new bottlenecks, and how to avoid adopting every shiny agent at once. Leave with a plan for building software with AI, not just a tool list.", "language": "en"},"DVT302-R1": {"abstract": "AI now touches every stage of software delivery: coding assistants, autonomous agents, modernization tools, security scanners, operations, and the list grows every week. Which do you actually use, and when? This open discussion helps you make sense of the SDLC tooling landscape. Bring your scenario and we'll talk through it together: when to hand work to an autonomous agent versus a deterministic tool, how to chain AI across design, code, review, and operations without creating new bottlenecks, and how to avoid adopting every shiny agent at once. Leave with a plan for building software with AI, not just a tool list.", "language": "en"},"DVT305-R": {"abstract": "In this workshop, build modern CI/CD pipelines using AWS CodePipeline through the AWS Management Console. Learn how to work with monorepos and branching strategies. Explore safe-deployment features including canary and linear traffic shifting, CloudWatch alarm gates for automatic rollback, stage-level conditions that run integration tests before promotion, and concurrent execution modes to improve pipeline performance. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"DVT305-R1": {"abstract": "In this workshop, build modern CI/CD pipelines using AWS CodePipeline through the AWS Management Console. Learn how to work with monorepos and branching strategies. Explore safe-deployment features including canary and linear traffic shifting, CloudWatch alarm gates for automatic rollback, stage-level conditions that run integration tests before promotion, and concurrent execution modes to improve pipeline performance. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"DVT306-R": {"abstract": "Releasing software shouldn't mean holding your breath. In this chalk talk, explore how AWS CodePipeline, CodeBuild, and AppConfig help you automate safe, hands-off deployments to Amazon EC2, ECS, and Lambda across accounts and Regions. Learn to apply canary and linear traffic-shifting strategies, wire CloudWatch alarms for automatic rollback, and use stage-level conditions to gate promotions on integration tests. Whether you're deploying a single service or orchestrating hundreds, leave with patterns for zero-downtime releases you can trust.", "language": "en"},"DVT306-R1": {"abstract": "Releasing software shouldn't mean holding your breath. In this chalk talk, explore how AWS CodePipeline, CodeBuild, and AppConfig help you automate safe, hands-off deployments to Amazon EC2, ECS, and Lambda across accounts and Regions. Learn to apply canary and linear traffic-shifting strategies, wire CloudWatch alarms for automatic rollback, and use stage-level conditions to gate promotions on integration tests. Whether you're deploying a single service or orchestrating hundreds, leave with patterns for zero-downtime releases you can trust.", "language": "en"},"DVT307": {"abstract": "Teams pour AI into code generation and wonder why they're not shipping faster. In reality, accelerating one stage just moves the bottleneck downstream. This session follows a single task across its entire lifecycle and shows where AI compounds. We'll design and build with Kiro, pay down tech debt with AWS Transform, catch vulnerabilities early with AWS Continuum (formerly known as AWS Security Agent), ship through safe CI/CD pipelines, and resolve production issues with AWS DevOps Agent—then feed what we learn back into the next task. Come see our vision for an SDLC where every stage moves at the same speed.", "language": "en"},"DVT308-R": {"abstract": "AWS CloudFormation lets you manage infrastructure as code with increasing confidence and speed, whether you're deploying directly or through AI agents. In this chalk talk, we dive into the capabilities that shorten your dev-test cycle and keep deployments safe: deploy in seconds with Express mode, catch errors before deployment with pre-deployment validation, author templates faster using the CloudFormation Language Server in your IDE, connect AI agents to CloudFormation with the Agent Skill and MCP Server, reorganize stacks without disruption using Stack Refactoring, and prevent accidental overwrites with drift-aware change sets. Bring your templates and your toughest questions, we'll workshop solutions live.", "language": "en"},"DVT308-R1": {"abstract": "AWS CloudFormation lets you manage infrastructure as code with increasing confidence and speed, whether you're deploying directly or through AI agents. In this chalk talk, we dive into the capabilities that shorten your dev-test cycle and keep deployments safe: deploy in seconds with Express mode, catch errors before deployment with pre-deployment validation, author templates faster using the CloudFormation Language Server in your IDE, connect AI agents to CloudFormation with the Agent Skill and MCP Server, reorganize stacks without disruption using Stack Refactoring, and prevent accidental overwrites with drift-aware change sets. Bring your templates and your toughest questions, we'll workshop solutions live.", "language": "en"},"DVT309-R": {"abstract": "Your inbox fills with runtime deprecation notices—hundreds of Lambda functions on Node.js 20, Python 3.10, and whatever's next. Handling them one by one drains capacity you'd rather spend on features. This hands-on workshop flips the script: instead of chasing individual apps, you'll modernize a fleet. Using AWS Transform custom, you'll run AWS-provided transformations to upgrade Lambda runtimes at scale, then stand up continuous modernization to scan repositories against a baseline, prioritize what's drifting, and open pull requests automatically. Turn a wall of end-of-support alerts into a queue of ready-to-merge PRs. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"DVT309-R1": {"abstract": "Your inbox fills with runtime deprecation notices—hundreds of Lambda functions on Node.js 20, Python 3.10, and whatever's next. Handling them one by one drains capacity you'd rather spend on features. This hands-on workshop flips the script: instead of chasing individual apps, you'll modernize a fleet. Using AWS Transform custom, you'll run AWS-provided transformations to upgrade Lambda runtimes at scale, then stand up continuous modernization to scan repositories against a baseline, prioritize what's drifting, and open pull requests automatically. Turn a wall of end-of-support alerts into a queue of ready-to-merge PRs. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"DVT310-R": {"abstract": "AI coding agents can write application logic, but can they provision your infrastructure too? In this chalk talk, explore AWS Cloud Development Kit's (AWS CDK) latest capabilities—Mixins, the CDK Toolkit Library, and programmatic synth/deploy APIs—make CDK a first-class target for agentic workflows. See how agents reason about constructs, compose infrastructure from natural-language intent, and validate results before deployment. We'll discuss patterns that work, guardrails that keep agents safe, and where human judgment still matters. Bring your questions—this is an interactive session for anyone curious about the intersection of AI agents and infrastructure as code.", "language": "en"},"DVT310-R1": {"abstract": "AI coding agents can write application logic, but can they provision your infrastructure too? In this chalk talk, explore AWS Cloud Development Kit's (AWS CDK) latest capabilities—Mixins, the CDK Toolkit Library, and programmatic synth/deploy APIs—make CDK a first-class target for agentic workflows. See how agents reason about constructs, compose infrastructure from natural-language intent, and validate results before deployment. We'll discuss patterns that work, guardrails that keep agents safe, and where human judgment still matters. Bring your questions—this is an interactive session for anyone curious about the intersection of AI agents and infrastructure as code.", "language": "en"},"DVT311": {"abstract": "In this workshop, build and deploy applications using infrastructure as code with the AWS Cloud Development Kit (AWS CDK). Create resources using familiar programming languages, apply CDK Mixins to compose reusable behaviors across constructs, and safely reorganize stacks with CDK Refactor. Learn maintenance and operations tips including cross-account output references and programmatic deployments with the CDK Toolkit Library. Get an introduction to building your own constructs. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"DVT312-R": {"abstract": "In this workshop, build and test AWS CloudFormation templates hands-on. Create stacks to deploy and manage resources, then use the CloudFormation Language Server to author with real-time validation in your IDE. Explore testing tools—cfn-lint, CloudFormation Guard, and early change-set validation—to catch errors before deployment. Learn language features like Stack Refactoring and drift-aware change sets that help you evolve templates safely over time. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"DVT312-R1": {"abstract": "In this workshop, build and test AWS CloudFormation templates hands-on. Create stacks to deploy and manage resources, then use the CloudFormation Language Server to author with real-time validation in your IDE. Explore testing tools—cfn-lint, CloudFormation Guard, and early change-set validation—to catch errors before deployment. Learn language features like Stack Refactoring and drift-aware change sets that help you evolve templates safely over time. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"DVT313": {"abstract": "Learn how to create applications that use real-time data to keep users engaged. In this workshop, build a multiplayer application with live updates and group chat functionality using AWS Amplify Gen 2. Define your backend—data model, auth rules, and real-time subscriptions—in TypeScript and deploy to a cloud sandbox in seconds. Connect your frontend to a serverless backend powered by AWS AppSync WebSockets. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"DVT314-R": {"abstract": "Kiro web and mobile bring agentic development to your browser—every session in an isolated cloud sandbox, no local setup, accessible anywhere you have an internet connection. In this hands-on workshop, you'll work with Kiro two ways. First, iterate interactively: connect a repo, explore the codebase, and shape changes in real time. Then go hands-off: assign a task from a tracked issue, let Kiro plan and implement autonomously, and review the pull request it opens. Sessions run in the cloud and keep going after you close your laptop—check in and review diffs from your phone with the Kiro iOS app. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"DVT314-R1": {"abstract": "Kiro web and mobile bring agentic development to your browser—every session in an isolated cloud sandbox, no local setup, accessible anywhere you have an internet connection. In this hands-on workshop, you'll work with Kiro two ways. First, iterate interactively: connect a repo, explore the codebase, and shape changes in real time. Then go hands-off: assign a task from a tracked issue, let Kiro plan and implement autonomously, and review the pull request it opens. Sessions run in the cloud and keep going after you close your laptop—check in and review diffs from your phone with the Kiro iOS app. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"DVT316": {"abstract": "Web developers are under pressure to deliver differentiating experiences to more users, faster than ever. Building secure, reliable, scalable applications at 10x speed takes solutions that let developers start easily, ship quickly, and iterate without friction. In this breakout, we'll share how AWS is evolving to meet the needs of modern full-stack developers from defining backends in code and connecting real-time data, to deploying and scaling globally with minimal overhead. Join us to see what's new and where full-stack development on AWS is headed.", "language": "en"},"DVT319": {"abstract": "Most developers use AI coding assistants as interactive tools and see modest gains. A small cohort works differently: they treat agents as autonomous workers they direct and validate, and the results are a step function in productivity improvements, not an increment. In this session, we break down what these power users do differently, share success stories, where experts still find friction, and the bottlenecks coming next. We'll share demos so you leave with concrete, repeatable practices for getting orders-of-magnitude more out of Kiro agents.", "language": "en"},"DVT320": {"abstract": "Learn how Kiro turns working sessions into durable knowledge and how to design, debug, and evaluate an agent’s memory lifecycle. See how Kiro extracts learnings from explicit instructions and task outcomes, such as successful build commands or rejected pull requests. Explore how it consolidates new knowledge with existing memories, resolves contradictions and expiring facts, and retrieves only relevant context for tasks. You’ll also learn how provenance keeps memories auditable and correctable, how memory stays consistent across IDE, CLI, and web, how Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Memory scopes knowledge globally or by repository, and how retrieval quality is measured by task outcomes.", "language": "en"},"DVT323-R": {"abstract": "We all have it: the monolith nobody wants to touch, and the pile of small apps and Lambda functions racking up end-of-life notices. This chalk talk is your support group. Bring your real modernization headaches and we'll discuss how AWS Transform tackles the whole spectrum. On one side, Transform custom handles the big, complex jobs: decomposing Java and .NET monoliths, translating languages, upgrading frameworks at scale. On the other, continuous modernization autonomously scans thousands of repositories, prioritizes what's drifting, and opens remediation pull requests on a schedule. Bring questions about your own portfolio and leave with a plan of attack.", "language": "en"},"DVT323-R1": {"abstract": "We all have it: the monolith nobody wants to touch, and the pile of small apps and Lambda functions racking up end-of-life notices. This chalk talk is your support group. Bring your real modernization headaches and we'll discuss how AWS Transform tackles the whole spectrum. On one side, Transform custom handles the big, complex jobs: decomposing Java and .NET monoliths, translating languages, upgrading frameworks at scale. On the other, continuous modernization autonomously scans thousands of repositories, prioritizes what's drifting, and opens remediation pull requests on a schedule. Bring questions about your own portfolio and leave with a plan of attack.", "language": "en"},"DVT326": {"abstract": "AI makes it possible to generate and test far more product variations than a team could create by hand. But running more experiments only helps if you can release each variation safely, measure the right signals, and separate meaningful results from noise. In this code-focused session, you’ll learn how to use AI and feature flags to control experiment variants, target audiences, adjust exposure, and shut down underperforming tests without redeploying. We’ll also examine how Amazon has recently used AI in experiments on Amazon.com to create variations and extract better signals from the results.", "language": "en"},"DVT328": {"abstract": "What if you could stand up a full application backend—database, auth, real-time messaging, file storage, even an AI agent—in TypeScript, running locally, with no AWS account required? AWS Blocks is an open-source framework where each \"Block\" bundles your app code, a local dev implementation, and production AWS infrastructure. Compose the Blocks you need, run one command to develop locally, then deploy the same code to AWS unchanged. Built for a world where AI agents write the code, Blocks ships steering files that guide agents to correct architecture from the start. In this lightning talk, see how fast full-stack can be.", "language": "en"},"DVT329": {"abstract": "Startups live and die by velocity. Small teams, big ambitions, and no time to waste. In this session, hear how fast-moving startups are using Kiro to punch above their weight: turning ideas into working software with spec-driven and agentic development, keeping a lean team focused on product instead of boilerplate, and shipping features that would normally demand far more engineers. We'll describe real patterns startups use to move from concept to production quickly, and share practical takeaways any team can apply. Whether you're a founder, an early engineer, or scaling quickly, learn how agentic development helps small teams build big.", "language": "en"},"DVT330": {"abstract": "The legacy estate has not gone away. Aging applications with undocumented business logic, outdated frameworks, and platforms nobody wants to touch remain a priority. Agentic tools have increased development velocity, but speed does not eliminate technical debt — it changes its shape. This compounding debt erodes productivity, weakens security posture, and inflates operational cost. Learn how AWS Transform custom and continuous modernization help organizations assess and remediate technical debt at scale — from framework migrations like Angular to React to language translations like C to Rust.", "language": "en"},"DVT331": {"abstract": "Kiro agents execute code, invoke models, and interact with external services on behalf of developers. Enterprise administrators desire to constrain what agents can do without slowing developers down. We built a centralized governance system backed by Dogwood/Cedar that compiles admin-authored policies into governance documents enforced across every client (IDE, CLI, Web,Mobile) and server-side services. The system separates governance from configuration, uses deny-wins semantics with fail-closed enforcement, and scopes rules by hierarchies. Learn how we use Kiro itself to author and analyze governance policies to make enterprise controls human-friendly and manageable.", "language": "en"},"DVT332-R": {"abstract": "Handing real work to a crew of agents raises real questions: How does it coordinate parallel subagents without losing the thread? How does memory save across sessions so agents don't start cold? How do you give agents access to your code and CI while staying safe? In this chalk talk, we'll dig into how Kiro Crew works: observable multi-agent orchestration over Agent Client Protocol, self-evolving memory and skills you can inspect and edit, scheduled and webhook-triggered jobs, and defense-in-depth security with sandboxing, denied-by-default commands, and signed audit logs. Bring your architecture and security questions and we'll whiteboard through them.", "language": "en"},"DVT332-R1": {"abstract": "Handing real work to a crew of agents raises real questions: How does it coordinate parallel subagents without losing the thread? How does memory save across sessions so agents don't start cold? How do you give agents access to your code and CI while staying safe? In this chalk talk, we'll dig into how Kiro Crew works: observable multi-agent orchestration over Agent Client Protocol, self-evolving memory and skills you can inspect and edit, scheduled and webhook-triggered jobs, and defense-in-depth security with sandboxing, denied-by-default commands, and signed audit logs. Bring your architecture and security questions and we'll whiteboard through them.", "language": "en"},"DVT332-R2": {"abstract": "Handing real work to a crew of agents raises real questions: How does it coordinate parallel subagents without losing the thread? How does memory save across sessions so agents don't start cold? How do you give agents access to your code and CI while staying safe? In this chalk talk, we'll dig into how Kiro Crew works: observable multi-agent orchestration over Agent Client Protocol, self-evolving memory and skills you can inspect and edit, scheduled and webhook-triggered jobs, and defense-in-depth security with sandboxing, denied-by-default commands, and signed audit logs. Bring your architecture and security questions and we'll whiteboard through them.", "language": "en"},"DVT333": {"abstract": "Building modern web and mobile front ends means juggling a lot of moving parts, and everyone's stack looks a little different. This chalk talk is an open Q&A, so bring your real questions and challenges. We'll talk through how AWS Amplify helps you define backends in code and ship full-stack apps fast, how AWS AppSync powers real-time and offline data with managed GraphQL and WebSocket APIs, and how AWS Device Farm lets you test across real browsers and mobile devices before your users do. Whether you're starting a greenfield app or scaling an existing one, come ready to steer the conversation.", "language": "en"},"DVT336-R": {"abstract": "AI coding agents are increasingly orchestrating cloud infrastructure, but they can be expensive and slow when every action requires chaining low-level API calls. The AWS CLI offers a different path: composable, structured commands that collapse multi-step workflows into single invocations. In this chalk talk, we'll explore how Agent Toolkit gives AI agents first-class access to AWS through the CLI, why purpose-built commands are inherently cheaper for agents to execute, and what \"developer experience for machines\" means for CLI design going forward.", "language": "en"},"DVT336-R1": {"abstract": "AI coding agents are increasingly orchestrating cloud infrastructure, but they can be expensive and slow when every action requires chaining low-level API calls. The AWS CLI offers a different path: composable, structured commands that collapse multi-step workflows into single invocations. In this chalk talk, we'll explore how Agent Toolkit gives AI agents first-class access to AWS through the CLI, why purpose-built commands are inherently cheaper for agents to execute, and what \"developer experience for machines\" means for CLI design going forward.", "language": "en"},"DVT337-R": {"abstract": "A SaaS control plane - tenant onboarding, billing, configuration management, agent provisioning is the most repeatable software every software vendor builds from scratch. In this builder's session, use Kiro specs to define the control plane requirements and let Kiro implement the scaffolding, then wire multi-tenant agent capabilities using Strands' open-source constructs. You'll build a working onboarding flow, a tenant configuration store, and a simple agent provisioning endpoint - all from a spec, not from scratch. Leave with a reusable starting point for your own control plane and a pattern for using Kiro and Strands together to accelerate platform development.", "language": "en"},"DVT337-R1": {"abstract": "A SaaS control plane - tenant onboarding, billing, configuration management, agent provisioning is the most repeatable software every software vendor builds from scratch. In this builder's session, use Kiro specs to define the control plane requirements and let Kiro implement the scaffolding, then wire multi-tenant agent capabilities using Strands' open-source constructs. You'll build a working onboarding flow, a tenant configuration store, and a simple agent provisioning endpoint - all from a spec, not from scratch. Leave with a reusable starting point for your own control plane and a pattern for using Kiro and Strands together to accelerate platform development.", "language": "en"},"DVT338": {"abstract": "As agents work on longer, more complex tasks, the context window fills up and the agent can lose track of its plan and execution steps. The manual setup required to get multi-agent orchestration to reliably complete these kinds of tasks can be complex and brittle. In this session, you'll learn best practices, tools, and patterns that power users have perfected to set up and scale your multi-agent workloads and take on hours-long tasks with confidence.", "language": "en"},"DVT339": {"abstract": "As agents work on longer, more complex tasks, the context window fills up and the agent can lose track of its plan and execution steps. The manual setup required to get multi-agent orchestration to reliably complete these kinds of tasks can be complex and brittle. In this chalk talk, we will walk through best practices, tools, and patterns that power users have perfected to set up and scale multi-agent workloads. Bring questions, challenges, or edge cases you ran into and we'll walk you through how to approach them to improve reliability.", "language": "en"},"DVT340": {"abstract": "Your AI agent writes code fast, but when you unleash it on a large codebase, context can fill up quickly and the agent can make questionable implementation choices. Learn how Kiro uses neurosymbolic techniques to understand what you want to build, removing ambiguity, contradictions, and gaps in your requirements before any code is generated. Then, learn simple practices you can use to keep your agent implementation surgical, and detect drift before it spreads all over your codebase.", "language": "en"},"DVT341": {"abstract": "Kiro Crew is the persistent, open source development workspace that remembers your context, learns how you work, and coordinates across your unique tools and workflows, so you come back to progress instead of another workflow to restart. In this session, you'll learn how to make Kiro Crew work for your team: wire in the tools your team already use over MCP, set up scheduled and webhook-triggered jobs so work keeps moving while you're away, and shape shared memory and reusable skills so agents don't start cold and maintain team standards and best practices.", "language": "en"},"DVT342-S": {"abstract": "10x developers may be a myth, but 10x organizations are very real, as proven by the influential study performed in the 1980s, ‘The Coding War Games.’ In this keynote-style session, teams will take a detailed, granular look at the barriers to productivity developers face today and modern approaches for removing them. You’ll learn about the history of productivity theory, and how previous practices have influenced our current understanding. DevOps and Platform Engineering philosophy is clearly converging on developer experience. But which of these approaches works? DORA? SPACE? DevEx? What should we invest in and create urgency behind today so we don’t have the same discussion again in a decade? This presentation is brought to you by Atlassian, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"DVT343": {"abstract": "Getting a web application live is fast. Keeping it running, reliable, and affordable as you grow is the hard part. In this chalk talk, we dig into the developer journey from first deploy to production scale: zero-config deployment, full-stack provisioning with open-source building blocks, and how to scale without starting over. Bring your questions about framework support, backend integration, and pricing.", "language": "en"},"DVT344": {"abstract": "Your agent can ship a web application in seconds. What happens after that is the hard part. In this session, learn how you and your agents get everything you need in one place, integrated and ready in seconds. We walk through how to go from code to production without configuring infrastructure, how the Agent Toolkit keeps deployments reliable, and how to scale without starting over.", "language": "en"},"DVT345": {"abstract": "Whether you're shipping a monolith or hundreds of microservices, every change needs to reach production safely and quickly. In this session, learn how to build a CI/CD pipeline with AWS CodePipeline and AWS CodeBuild using infrastructure as code. The pipeline will utilize AI agents running on Amazon Bedrock to review every change—pull requests and direct commits alike—for security flaws, code quality, architecture, and accessibility. The agents report what they find, while you write rules to determine whether ship...or stop. AWS CodeDeploy will orchestrate canary deployments that roll back automatically when an issue is detected by an Amazon CloudWatch alarm.", "language": "en"},"DVT346": {"abstract": "AI agents build applications differently than people do. They work from code, call APIs, run in parallel, and keep working after you close your laptop. In this session, learn what a backend has to provide for an agent to carry an application from first prompt to production: schema and configuration as code the agent can read and change, serverless infrastructure with nothing to size or provision, data APIs and authentication that work on the first call, isolated environments for every experiment the agent runs, logs the agent can query when something breaks, and approval steps that keep destructive changes in your hands.", "language": "en"},"DVT405": {"abstract": "Your AI agent writes code fast, but is it building what you actually meant? Kiro's requirements analysis goes far beyond asking an LLM to generate a spec. It combines auto-formalization, SMT solvers, and neuro-symbolic techniques to mathematically detect ambiguity, contradictions, and gaps in your requirements before any code is generated. In this session, learn how we turned decades of formal methods into a developer experience that feels like answering simple yes/no questions while catching the bugs that cost the most to fix.", "language": "en"},"DVT406-R": {"abstract": "Anyone can prompt an agent. The hard part is getting it to work the way you want—for hours, unattended, without going off the rails. This advanced workshop digs into orchestration. You'll build a custom agent, then set a goal so strong the agent can't redefine it or declare victory early. You'll decompose work across subagents, use directives like /goal to keep execution on track, and design guardrails that let an agent run long and autonomously while landing where you intended. We'll use Kiro, but these patterns transfer to any agentic system. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"DVT406-R1": {"abstract": "Anyone can prompt an agent. The hard part is getting it to work the way you want—for hours, unattended, without going off the rails. This advanced workshop digs into orchestration. You'll build a custom agent, then set a goal so strong the agent can't redefine it or declare victory early. You'll decompose work across subagents, use directives like /goal to keep execution on track, and design guardrails that let an agent run long and autonomously while landing where you intended. We'll use Kiro, but these patterns transfer to any agentic system. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"DVT409-R": {"abstract": "Kiro Crew is an open-source, multi-agent workspace you can bend to your own workflows. In this hands-on workshop, you'll put it to work. Hand your crew a real task and watching it plan, spawn parallel subagents, and gate each action for your approval. Then personalize: wire in the tools you already use over MCP, set up scheduled and webhook-triggered jobs so work keeps moving while you're away, and shape memory and reusable skills so agents don't start cold. Finally, assemble a purpose-built App to run a workflow end-to-end, from intake through review. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"DVT409-R1": {"abstract": "Kiro Crew is an open-source, multi-agent workspace you can bend to your own workflows. In this hands-on workshop, you'll put it to work. Hand your crew a real task and watching it plan, spawn parallel subagents, and gate each action for your approval. Then personalize: wire in the tools you already use over MCP, set up scheduled and webhook-triggered jobs so work keeps moving while you're away, and shape memory and reusable skills so agents don't start cold. Finally, assemble a purpose-built App to run a workflow end-to-end, from intake through review. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"DVT409-R2": {"abstract": "Kiro Crew is an open-source, multi-agent workspace you can bend to your own workflows. In this hands-on workshop, you'll put it to work. Hand your crew a real task and watching it plan, spawn parallel subagents, and gate each action for your approval. Then personalize: wire in the tools you already use over MCP, set up scheduled and webhook-triggered jobs so work keeps moving while you're away, and shape memory and reusable skills so agents don't start cold. Finally, assemble a purpose-built App to run a workflow end-to-end, from intake through review. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"DVT413-R": {"abstract": "AI coding agents sped up code generation, but not how fast it reaches customers. Teams seeing 4.5x to 10x gains aren't just adopting tools. They've redesigned how they work. In this chalk talk, learn what these \"frontier teams\" do differently: investing in agent context and steering files, making intent explicit before code is written, feeding agents well-scoped work instead of babysitting them, shifting testing left so agents self-correct before they reach the pipeline. We'll unpack real examples from teams across Amazon and talk through how to run your own pilot. Bring your questions about making AI work in your organization.", "language": "en"},"DVT413-R1": {"abstract": "AI coding agents sped up code generation, but not how fast it reaches customers. Teams seeing 4.5x to 10x gains aren't just adopting tools. They've redesigned how they work. In this chalk talk, learn what these \"frontier teams\" do differently: investing in agent context and steering files, making intent explicit before code is written, feeding agents well-scoped work instead of babysitting them, shifting testing left so agents self-correct before they reach the pipeline. We'll unpack real examples from teams across Amazon and talk through how to run your own pilot. Bring your questions about making AI work in your organization.", "language": "en"},"DVT413-R2": {"abstract": "AI coding agents sped up code generation, but not how fast it reaches customers. Teams seeing 4.5x to 10x gains aren't just adopting tools. They've redesigned how they work. In this chalk talk, learn what these \"frontier teams\" do differently: investing in agent context and steering files, making intent explicit before code is written, feeding agents well-scoped work instead of babysitting them, shifting testing left so agents self-correct before they reach the pipeline. We'll unpack real examples from teams across Amazon and talk through how to run your own pilot. Bring your questions about making AI work in your organization.", "language": "en"},"DVT415-R": {"abstract": "The best results from agentic AI don't come from clever one-off prompts, but from shaping how agents work. In this session, go beyond prompting to learn techniques that apply to any agentic coding tool. Craft reusable skills that encode your team's know-how, build custom agents tuned to specific jobs, and use steering files to carry your conventions and architecture across sessions. We'll cover how to set goals the agent will actually follow, when to let it run autonomously, and how to tell a skill that's useful versus noise. We'll demo live in Kiro, but the principles transfer wherever you work.", "language": "en"},"DVT415-R1": {"abstract": "The best results from agentic AI don't come from clever one-off prompts, but from shaping how agents work. In this session, go beyond prompting to learn techniques that apply to any agentic coding tool. Craft reusable skills that encode your team's know-how, build custom agents tuned to specific jobs, and use steering files to carry your conventions and architecture across sessions. We'll cover how to set goals the agent will actually follow, when to let it run autonomously, and how to tell a skill that's useful versus noise. We'll demo live in Kiro, but the principles transfer wherever you work.", "language": "en"},"DVT415-R2": {"abstract": "The best results from agentic AI don't come from clever one-off prompts, but from shaping how agents work. In this session, go beyond prompting to learn techniques that apply to any agentic coding tool. Craft reusable skills that encode your team's know-how, build custom agents tuned to specific jobs, and use steering files to carry your conventions and architecture across sessions. We'll cover how to set goals the agent will actually follow, when to let it run autonomously, and how to tell a skill that's useful versus noise. We'll demo live in Kiro, but the principles transfer wherever you work.", "language": "en"},"GHJ201-R": {"abstract": "Experience a dynamic simulation of leading a digital transformation initiative. Navigate cloud migration, security automation, and operational excellence challenges. Architect secure cloud environments while balancing stakeholder needs and compliance. Compete to optimize speed, security, and innovation amid real-world obstacles. Gain actionable insights into cloud security governance and risk-aligned architecture.", "language": "en"},"GHJ201-R1": {"abstract": "Experience a dynamic simulation of leading a digital transformation initiative. Navigate cloud migration, security automation, and operational excellence challenges. Architect secure cloud environments while balancing stakeholder needs and compliance. Compete to optimize speed, security, and innovation amid real-world obstacles. Gain actionable insights into cloud security governance and risk-aligned architecture.", "language": "en"},"GHJ203-R": {"abstract": "Quantum computing is advancing—is your cryptographic infrastructure ready? In this immersive tabletop, executives will confront the urgent reality that current public-key cryptographic standards face obsolescence. Make high-stakes decisions on migration timelines, technology selection, and stakeholder coordination as quantum threats loom over customer data, transaction systems, and competitive intelligence. Balance budget constraints against discovery, risk prioritization, and implementation speed while navigating regulatory requirements and operational continuity. Compete to build quantum-resistant infrastructure before adversaries exploit today's vulnerabilities. Leave with actionable strategies for leading a post-quantum cryptography migration that protects trust and secures competitive advantage.", "language": "en"},"GHJ203-R1": {"abstract": "Quantum computing is advancing—is your cryptographic infrastructure ready? In this immersive tabletop, executives will confront the urgent reality that current public-key cryptographic standards face obsolescence. Make high-stakes decisions on migration timelines, technology selection, and stakeholder coordination as quantum threats loom over customer data, transaction systems, and competitive intelligence. Balance budget constraints against discovery, risk prioritization, and implementation speed while navigating regulatory requirements and operational continuity. Compete to build quantum-resistant infrastructure before adversaries exploit today's vulnerabilities. Leave with actionable strategies for leading a post-quantum cryptography migration that protects trust and secures competitive advantage.", "language": "en"},"GHJ205-R": {"abstract": "Embark on \"Third Party Risk Management,\" an engaging tabletop designed to challenge and refine your approach to managing external partnerships and vendor relationships. In this immersive experience, leaders are thrust into a high-stakes environment where they must navigate complex scenarios involving third-party risks. This tabletop not only enhances your ability to manage third-party risks effectively but also equips you with practical insights and strategies to safeguard your organization’s interests and strengthen its external partnerships. Join us to elevate your risk management acumen and drive resilient, forward-thinking approaches in an interconnected business landscape.", "language": "en"},"GHJ205-R1": {"abstract": "Embark on \"Third Party Risk Management,\" an engaging tabletop designed to challenge and refine your approach to managing external partnerships and vendor relationships. In this immersive experience, leaders are thrust into a high-stakes environment where they must navigate complex scenarios involving third-party risks. This tabletop not only enhances your ability to manage third-party risks effectively but also equips you with practical insights and strategies to safeguard your organization’s interests and strengthen its external partnerships. Join us to elevate your risk management acumen and drive resilient, forward-thinking approaches in an interconnected business landscape.", "language": "en"},"GHJ207-R": {"abstract": "Navigate AI-driven security threats as they escalate in real time. In this immersive executive tabletop, participants allocate a constrained governance budget, then respond to live crisis events scored across six dimensions. Practice navigating high-stakes scenarios including autonomous AI deployment failures, third-party security incidents, and emerging regulatory obligations. Confront real attack techniques, compliance frameworks, and enterprise liability exposure while aligning cross-functional teams under pressure. This experience sharpens governance instincts and crisis fluency—equipping leaders to govern Agentic AI risks, enforce accountability, and make decisive resource allocation calls before a real crisis demands it.", "language": "en"},"GHJ207-R1": {"abstract": "Navigate AI-driven security threats as they escalate in real time. In this immersive executive tabletop, participants allocate a constrained governance budget, then respond to live crisis events scored across six dimensions. Practice navigating high-stakes scenarios including autonomous AI deployment failures, third-party security incidents, and emerging regulatory obligations. Confront real attack techniques, compliance frameworks, and enterprise liability exposure while aligning cross-functional teams under pressure. This experience sharpens governance instincts and crisis fluency—equipping leaders to govern Agentic AI risks, enforce accountability, and make decisive resource allocation calls before a real crisis demands it.", "language": "en"},"GHJ207-R2": {"abstract": "Navigate AI-driven security threats as they escalate in real time. In this immersive executive tabletop, participants allocate a constrained governance budget, then respond to live crisis events scored across six dimensions. Practice navigating high-stakes scenarios including autonomous AI deployment failures, third-party security incidents, and emerging regulatory obligations. Confront real attack techniques, compliance frameworks, and enterprise liability exposure while aligning cross-functional teams under pressure. This experience sharpens governance instincts and crisis fluency—equipping leaders to govern Agentic AI risks, enforce accountability, and make decisive resource allocation calls before a real crisis demands it.", "language": "en"},"GHJ210-R": {"abstract": "Join the \"Supply Chain Incident Response\" tabletop exercise, where executive leaders tackle supply chain disruptions in a realistic, interactive simulation. Collaborate to develop and execute strategies for managing crises, explore decision-making and risk assessment, and enhance communication skills. This exercise strengthens your organization's preparedness and resilience, helping you safeguard operations and improve response capabilities. Build robust strategies to handle and recover from supply chain disruptions effectively.", "language": "en"},"GHJ210-R1": {"abstract": "Join the \"Supply Chain Incident Response\" tabletop exercise, where executive leaders tackle supply chain disruptions in a realistic, interactive simulation. Collaborate to develop and execute strategies for managing crises, explore decision-making and risk assessment, and enhance communication skills. This exercise strengthens your organization's preparedness and resilience, helping you safeguard operations and improve response capabilities. Build robust strategies to handle and recover from supply chain disruptions effectively.", "language": "en"},"GHJ212": {"abstract": "Coordinating agents is where multi agent systems break. They duplicate work, drop handoffs, and burn tokens. In this workshop, build and deploy five autonomous agents on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore using Kiro and the Strands Agents SDK, then field them five versus five in live Agentic Football matches. Write the tools your agents call, orchestrate multi turn reasoning across a maze like arena, and solve coordination problems that decide the match. Tune prompts, roles, and tactics between rounds and watch your score climb a live leaderboard. Leave with working code, production ready multi agent patterns, and a shot at the Finale.", "language": "en"},"GHJ213": {"abstract": "Experience the ultimate AI showdown from your theater seat as fifty finalists from the AWS AI League season battle head-to-head in real time for the $50,000 championship prize. This electrifying viewing experience offers ML practitioners, developers, and AI enthusiasts unique insights into agent orchestration, model customization, and multi-agent coordination at scale, as five-versus-five AI agent squads face off live in Agentic Football, built on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore and the Strands Agents SDK.", "language": "en"},"GHJ301-S": {"abstract": "Join us for an immersive, collaborative learning exercise to build practical skills in deploying and monitoring AI-powered applications on AWS. Dive into a live environment where you'll engineer production-ready AI systems and ensure reliability through comprehensive observability. Leverage Amazon Bedrock, Amazon Bedrock Agentcore, foundation models, AI agents, prompt engineering, model evaluation, and ML pipeline monitoring as you build and operate AI workloads end-to-end. Face challenges demanding both engineering excellence and operational awareness for AI systems in production! Whether you're an experienced ML engineer or just starting to build with AI, this GameDay will put your skills to the ultimate test. Come solo or bring a group. Either way, you'll compete on a team. This session features integrated use cases with New Relic. Walk-ups welcome.", "language": "en"},"GHJ302-S": {"abstract": "Join us for an immersive, collaborative exercise to develop practical skills in data protection and automated disaster recovery on AWS. Dive into a live environment where you'll design backup strategies, recovery procedures, and resilience architectures that keep workloads safe and recoverable. Leverage cross-region replication, recovery point and time objectives, and automated failover as you protect workloads. Face challenges that simulate real-world disasters where every second of downtime counts! Whether you're a seasoned systems architect or new to disaster recovery, this GameDay will put your resilience skills to the ultimate test. Come solo or bring a group. Either way, you'll compete on a team. This session features integrated use cases with Clumio by Commvault. Walk-ups welcome.", "language": "en"},"GHJ303-S": {"abstract": "Join us for an immersive, collaborative exercise to develop practical skills in maximizing developer productivity on AWS. Dive into a live environment where you'll streamline development workflows, optimize CI/CD pipelines, and accelerate software delivery using modern tools and practices. Leverage build automation, deployment pipelines, code quality, and performance optimization as you race to ship features faster without sacrificing quality. Face challenges that push your ability to eliminate bottlenecks, automate toil, and deliver software at speed! Whether you're a seasoned DevOps engineer or a developer looking to level up, this GameDay will put your productivity skills to the ultimate test. Come solo or bring a group. Either way, you'll compete on a team. This session features integrated use cases with Dynatrace. Walk-ups welcome.", "language": "en"},"GHJ304-S": {"abstract": "Join us for a collaborative learning exercise to develop practical skills in platform engineering and cloud-native architecture on AWS. Dive into a live AWS environment where you will build and operate internal developer platforms, design cloud-native applications, and implement container orchestration at scale. Build skills in containerization, container orchestration platforms, service mesh, platform APIs, and self-service infrastructure as you construct golden paths that empower development teams. Face scenarios that challenge your ability to balance developer experience with operational governance! Whether you are a seasoned platform engineer or just exploring cloud-native patterns, this GameDay will put your skills to the ultimate test. Come solo or bring a group. Either way, you'll compete on a team. This session features integrated use cases with Red Hat. Walk-ups welcome.", "language": "en"},"GHJ305": {"abstract": "Join us for an immersive, collaborative exercise to develop practical skills in application security on AWS. Dive into a live environment where you'll configure security agents, launch AI-driven penetration tests, and remediate critical vulnerabilities before the biggest production launch of the year for Unicorn.Rentals. Build skills in agent-based security assessment, OWASP Top 10 vulnerability identification, and shift-left security practices. Every unpatched vulnerability is an open door. You and your AI-powered security agent are the only things standing between your application and compromise! Whether you're a security engineer, DevOps practitioner, developer, or cloud architect, security is your responsibility, and this GameDay will put your skills to the ultimate test. Come solo or bring a group. Either way, you'll compete on a team. Walk-ups welcome.", "language": "en"},"GHJ306-S": {"abstract": "Join us for a collaborative learning exercise to develop practical skills in operational excellence and service reliability on AWS. Dive into a live AWS environment where you will build and maintain highly reliable services while responding to incidents, managing deployments, and ensuring system health at scale. Build skills in monitoring, incident response, runbook automation, deployment strategies, and SLO management as you keep critical services running under pressure. Face scenarios that test your ability to detect, diagnose, and resolve operational issues before they impact customers! Whether you are a seasoned SRE or just exploring operations, this GameDay will put your skills to the ultimate test. Come solo or bring a group. Either way, you'll compete on a team. This session features integrated use cases with Datadog. Walk-ups welcome.", "language": "en"},"GHJ307-S": {"abstract": "Join us for a collaborative learning exercise to develop practical skills in cybersecurity and threat detection on AWS. Dive into a live AWS environment where you will hunt for threats, analyze attack patterns, and defend your cloud workloads against sophisticated adversaries in real time. Build skills in threat intelligence, security analytics, and automated threat remediation as you protect your environment from active cyber threats. Face off against real-world attack scenarios with multi-stage threat patterns that will test your ability to detect and respond under pressure! Whether you are a seasoned security analyst or just exploring cybersecurity, this GameDay will sharpen your skills. Come solo or bring a group. Either way, you'll compete on a team. This session features integrated use cases with SentinelOne. Walk-ups welcome.", "language": "en"},"GHJ308-S-R": {"abstract": "Come with your team of 2 or join one on the spot. Compete head-to-head, race the leaderboard, and win prizes. Solve real-world Gen AI and Agentic AI scenarios using Amazon Bedrock, SageMaker AI & Kiro - including a sponsored challenge from NVIDIA to build and optimize GenAI workloads using NVIDIA's accelerated computing and AI inference stack on AWS. Challenges suit all skill levels, with AWS experts and helpful clues throughout. All you need: a laptop, curiosity, and a competitive spirit.", "language": "en"},"GHJ308-S-R1": {"abstract": "Come with your team of four or join a team and meet new friends in the session. Are you ready to build and deploy agentic AI solutions on AWS? We provide hands-on environments where you can architect intelligent and production-ready agents. How do you orchestrate multi-step agent reasoning with tool use? How can you design agents that plan, act, and self-correct autonomously? Whether you are new to AWS or a seasoned Cloud Ninja, this Jam is your chance to demonstrate your skills and learn new ones. Structured for all experience levels, our sessions include guided exercises, helpful clues, and support from AWS experts to accelerate your agentic AI expertise. Team up, compete with others, climb the leaderboard, and win prizes based on speed and accuracy. With expert guidance every step of the way, all you need is a computer, a desire to learn, and a competitive spirit.", "language": "en"},"GHJ309-S": {"abstract": "Come with your team of 2 or join one on the spot. Compete head-to-head, race the leaderboard, and win prizes. Tackle hands-on DevOps scenarios to modernize architectures and implement best practices - including a sponsored challenge from LaunchDarkly to ship safely with feature flags and progressive delivery. Whether you're new to AWS or a seasoned Cloud Ninja, guided exercises and AWS experts support all levels. All you need: a laptop, curiosity, and a competitive spirit.", "language": "en"},"GHJ310-S": {"abstract": "Come with your team of 2 or join one on the spot. Compete head-to-head, race the leaderboard, and win prizes. Work through simulated incident response scenarios - including a sponsored challenge from Fortinet to test your skills securing cloud workloads with Fortinet's network and cloud security platform. Can you find the breach and prevent the next one? AWS experts and guided exercises support all levels. All you need: a laptop, curiosity, and a competitive spirit.", "language": "en"},"GHJ311": {"abstract": "Come with your team of 2 or join one on the spot. Compete head-to-head, race the leaderboard, and win prizes. Dive deep into AWS with our All-In session — hands-on environments covering AI-powered solutions, DevOps automation, and cloud security. Explore cutting-edge AI services, streamline deployments, and implement security best practices. Challenges are designed for all skill levels, with helpful clues and AWS expert support throughout. Work solo or as a team on real-world scenarios, expand your AWS expertise, and connect with fellow cloud enthusiasts. All you need: a laptop, curiosity, and a competitive spirit.", "language": "en"},"GHJ312": {"abstract": "Come with your team of 2 or join one on the spot. Compete head-to-head, race the leaderboard, and win prizes. Tackle hands-on DevOps environments to improve applications and modernize architectures using multiple AWS services. What are the limitations of the current solution? How can you do it better? Whether you're new to AWS or a seasoned Cloud Ninja, this is your chance to demonstrate and grow your skills. Guided exercises, helpful clues, and AWS expert support throughout. All you need: a laptop, a desire to learn, and a competitive spirit.", "language": "en"},"GHJ313-S": {"abstract": "Come with your team of 2 or join one on the spot. Compete head-to-head, race the leaderboard, and win prizes. Work through simulated incident response scenarios - including a sponsored challenge from Semgrep to find and fix code vulnerabilities early using Semgrep's static analysis and code security scanning. Can you find the breach and prevent the next one? AWS experts and guided exercises support all levels. All you need: a laptop, curiosity, and a competitive spirit.", "language": "en"},"GHJ314": {"abstract": "Turn a generic model into one that wins on your task, and prove it with data. In this hands-on workshop, you customize a foundation model on Amazon SageMaker AI using supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR), with no infrastructure to manage. Then you build an agent on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore that orchestrates the model end to end: preparing datasets, triggering training, registering models, evaluating with LLM-as-a-Judge, and deploying to a real-time endpoint. Your score posts to a live leaderboard, and you tune between rounds to climb it. You walk away knowing when customization beats a strong prompt.", "language": "en"},"GHJ315": {"abstract": "In this hands-on workshop you’ll compete head-to-head with other builders to build your own domain specific AI. You’ll tackle real-world challenges as you generate synthetic datasets, experiment with model customization, and fine-tune models to improve accuracy, clarity, and relevance. You’ll learn just how accessible and cost-effective AI customization can be. Whether you’re chasing the championship or simply sharpening your AI expertise, this session delivers practical techniques you can apply immediately. You must bring your laptop to participate", "language": "en"},"GHJ317-S": {"abstract": "Come with your team of 2 or join one on the spot. Compete head-to-head, race the leaderboard, and win prizes. Tackle AI, DevOps, and security scenarios across AWS services - including a sponsored challenge from Vonage to add real-time voice, video, and messaging to applications using Vonage communications APIs. Challenges suit all skill levels, with AWS expert guidance and helpful clues throughout. All you need: a laptop, curiosity, and a competitive spirit.", "language": "en"},"GHJ318-R": {"abstract": "Ever stared at multiple disconnected systems held together by manual handoffs and thought, \"there has to be a better way\"? Agentic AI is that better way. In this hands-on Rodeo session, you team up to design an end-to-end agentic solution for a real-world scenario: multi-agent orchestration, human-in-the-loop decision points, and MCP integration. Swap ideas with other teams, pressure-test your architecture with expert coaches, and walk away with reusable patterns for turning manual chaos into autonomous workflows. Bring your A-game, the best design rides off with the win.", "language": "en"},"GHJ318-R1": {"abstract": "Ever stared at multiple disconnected systems held together by manual handoffs and thought, \"there has to be a better way\"? Agentic AI is that better way. In this hands-on Rodeo session, you team up to design an end-to-end agentic solution for a real-world scenario: multi-agent orchestration, human-in-the-loop decision points, and MCP integration. Swap ideas with other teams, pressure-test your architecture with expert coaches, and walk away with reusable patterns for turning manual chaos into autonomous workflows. Bring your A-game, the best design rides off with the win.", "language": "en"},"GHJ319-R": {"abstract": "Everyone wants AI. Nobody wants to be the team that shipped it without security and governance. In this Rodeo, you'll join a team and face three demanding stakeholders: a security hawk, a data visibility champion, and a platform lead, racing to get Agentic AI live. Your team's mission: build a governance architecture that keeps all three happy. Together, you'll whiteboard, debate, and build against the clock, pressure-test your architecture with expert coaches, and walk away with reusable patterns. Best architecture rides away with the win.", "language": "en"},"GHJ319-R1": {"abstract": "Everyone wants AI. Nobody wants to be the team that shipped it without security and governance. In this Rodeo, you'll join a team and face three demanding stakeholders: a security hawk, a data visibility champion, and a platform lead, racing to get Agentic AI live. Your team's mission: build a governance architecture that keeps all three happy. Together, you'll whiteboard, debate, and build against the clock, pressure-test your architecture with expert coaches, and walk away with reusable patterns. Best architecture rides away with the win.", "language": "en"},"GHJ320": {"abstract": "Your data estate wasn't designed for AI agents. Nobody's was. But somewhere between your RDBMS, NoSQL clusters, and that untouchable data lake, an agentic-ready architecture waits to be built. In this Rodeo session, your team draws a challenge: a legacy warehouse needing a semantic layer, a sprawling lake needing an ontology, or a metadata mess that lets agents in without letting governance out. Your job: build a data layer that makes agents productive, keeps compliance calm, and gives analysts self-service. You'll whiteboard ontologies, debate metadata, and wire up multi-agent access against the clock. Best architecture rides away with the win.", "language": "en"},"GHJ321": {"abstract": "\"Your call is important to us.\" Nobody believes that anymore, and neither do your customers. Contact centers are being rebuilt around AI, and getting the architecture right is what separates the experiences people love from the ones they dread. In this Rodeo session, you'll join a team and design the transformation yourself: voice AI that understands people, routing that knows why they're calling, and handoffs to humans that don't start with \"can you repeat your issue?\" You'll whiteboard, swap ideas with teammates, and build against the clock, pressure-test your architecture with expert coaches, and walk away with reusable patterns. Saddle up! The design that finally makes \"your call is important to us\" true rides off with the win.", "language": "en"},"HMC202": {"abstract": "While most workloads can run in the cloud, some remain on-premises or at the edge due to low latency, local data processing, or data residency needs. AWS Local Zones, AWS Outposts, AWS Dedicated Local Zones, and AWS AI Factories extend AWS services, APIs, and tools to metro areas, on-premises data centers, and the far edge. Explore how these infrastructure offerings power a broad range of hybrid cloud and edge computing use cases such as financial trading, robotic control, and manufacturing. Learn how AWS can help you accelerate migration and modernization of virtualized, containerized, and bare-metal workloads with on-premises dependencies, meet digital sovereignty needs for regulated workloads, and run generative AI applications with on-premises and edge data.", "language": "en"},"HMC203-S": {"abstract": "Discover how Intel's custom SAP-certified Intel® Xeon® 6 processor, exclusively available on AWS, transforms and accelerates your SAP RISE journey. Engineered to deliver the highest performance and fastest memory bandwidth among comparable Intel cloud processors, this powerful solution unlocks up to 50% higher SAPS performance. Whether migrating, consolidating, or scaling SAP workloads, Intel Xeon 6 on AWS empowers organizations to optimize costs, enhance efficiency, and maximize their SAP RISE investment — driving faster, smarter, and more cost-effective outcomes for modern enterprise demands. This presentation is brought to you by Intel, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"HMC204-S": {"abstract": "IBM Power organizations support mission-critical workloads across industries. Now, there are more infrastructure options than ever before, providing hybrid transformation opportunities and challenges. This session explores how to develop a hybrid strategy that balances performance, resiliency, security, and business objectives. Hear how enterprises are exploring a phased modernization strategy that rehosts IBM workloads in a hybrid cloud environment, connects to AWS, and evolves their infrastructure. Learn how they establish secure, low-latency AWS connectivity and integrate IBM business logic with cloud-native services. This session provides proven frameworks, architectural insights, and modernization strategies to reduce risk, accelerate transformation, and maximize infrastructure value. This presentation is brought to you by Lightedge, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"HMC205-S": {"abstract": "For decades, NOCs have been built around dashboards, alerts, and humans manually stitching together signals from dozens of disconnected tools. That model is breaking under the complexity of modern hybrid networks and sprawling multi-cloud estates. Simply bolting AI onto existing monitoring tools isn't enough to fix it. This talk reimagines the NOC around purpose-built agents built for cloud-scale operations, each progressing through a five-stage maturity model (Observe, Monitor, Triage, Optimize, and Act), that governs how much autonomy each agent earns over time. Attendees will leave with a concrete mental model for evolving their own cloud operations stack from tools that observe to agents that act. This presentation is brought to you by Selector AI, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"HMC206-S": {"abstract": "AI workloads can consume millions of dollars in infrastructure before anyone notices whether they're being used efficiently. GPUs sit idle, clusters become overprovisioned, and engineering teams struggle to balance performance with rapidly growing costs. In this session, learn how engineering teams running Amazon EKS can identify wasted GPU capacity, understand the true cost of AI workloads, and automate optimization without sacrificing application performance. We'll share practical techniques to improve utilization, reduce waste, and optimize AI infrastructure at scale. This presentation is brought to you by Apptio, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"}};
  window.APP_DATA.sessions.forEach(function (s) {
    var x = m[s.session_id];
    if (x) for (var k in x) if (x[k]) s[k] = x[k];
  });
})();
